.TH IOCTL 2
.SH NAME
ioctl, stty, gtty \- control device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sgtty.h>
.PP
.B int ioctl(int fildes, int request, char *argp);
.PP
.B int stty(int fildes, struct sgttyb *argp);
.PP
.B int gtty(int fildes, struct sgttyb *argp);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ioctl
performs a variety of functions
on character special files (devices).
The writeups of various devices
in section 4 discuss how
.I ioctl
applies to them.
.PP
For certain status setting and status inquiries
about terminal devices, the functions
.I stty
and
.I gtty
are equivalent to
.RS
.B ioctl(fildes, TIOCSETP, argp)
.br
.B ioctl(fildes, TIOCGETP, argp)
.RE
.LP
respectively; see
.IR tty (4).
.PP
The following two calls, however, apply to any open file:
.PP
.RS
.B ioctl(fildes, FIOCLEX, NULL);
.br
.B ioctl(fildes, FIONCLEX, NULL);
.RE
.LP
The first causes the file to be closed automatically during
a successful
.I exec
operation;
the second reverses the effect of the first.
.PP
.B Note:
.B stty and gtty are real system calls here, not the ioctl write-arounds v7 made
.BR them .
They have gate numbers of their own and generated stubs of their own, and have
had since the syscall stubs first landed.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
stty(1), tty(4), exec(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Zero is returned if the call was successful;
\-1 if the file descriptor does not refer to
the kind of file for which it was intended.
.SH BUGS
Strictly speaking,
since 
.I ioctl
may be extended in different ways to devices with
different properties,
.I argp
should have an open-ended declaration like
.IP
.B union { struct sgttyb
.RB ... ;
\&...
.B } *argp;
.PP
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B struct sgttyb is five words \- four chars, each occupying a word of its own,
.B and an int.
A
.B char
struct member takes a whole word here, so nothing about that structure is packed
and there is no unpacking to do.
.PP
.B Note:
under b6sim,
.I ioctl
is an unconditional no-op that answers success and changes nothing, while the
real console is ECHO|CRMOD|XTABS.  A program whose output depends on tty modes
therefore cannot have one expectation for both harnesses; the curses tests split
along exactly that line.
.PP
The important thing is that the size is fixed by `struct sgttyb'.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_ioctl,
.B SYS_stty,
.B SYS_gtty
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
